<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdi-Heba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdi-Heba was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, called Urusalim, in the mid-14th century BCE, known from six of his letters to the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. He presented himself as a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, complained of rival rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labayu">Labayu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> and of the Habiru, and repeatedly asked for Egyptian troops. His letters are the earliest written evidence of Jerusalem as a political centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdi-heba.jpg" alt="Abdi-Heba" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_of_the_Amarna_letters._A_letter_from_Abdi-Heba_of_Jerusalem_to_the_Egyptian_Pharaoh_Amenhotep_III._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._From_Tell_el-Amarna,_Egypt._Vorderasiatisches_Museum,_Berlin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
